AB  - Indoor air quality (IAQ) and thermal comfort significantly influence occupants, who spend most of their time indoors. Climate-adaptive façades (CAFs) can modify their configurations in response to external conditions, and contribute significantly to improving and maintaining a desirable IAQ and thermal comfort. In response to contemporary CAF systems, which tend to generalize comfort levels, this paper addresses the difficulties of accommodating individual comfort preferences. To this end, we propose a novel workflow for training reinforcement learning (RL) controllers tailored for high-performance CAFs that incorporate fine-tuning mechanisms. RL has been widely adopted as a control strategy for various building components and equipment, such as HVAC systems. However, the application of RL to CAFs remains underexplored. Existing RL controllers for the built environment are typically trained on generalized comfort models, which can lead to incompatibilities between human self-determined operation of environment control systems based on variable personal comfort perceptions and digitally predetermined optimum values. This misalignment can result in occupant discomfort and increase energy consumption. In this paper, we conducted an analysis of a naturally ventilated building, and present the results demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed workflow in aligning an RL-based control system for CAFs with individual occupant comfort preferences. © 2025 and published by the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong.
