AB  - Against the backdrop of energy crises and climate change, dynamic facades have increasingly garnered attention. Dynamic facades are capable of responding to changes in the outdoor climate environment, thereby achieving the effect of regulating the indoor lighting and thermal environments. Dynamic facades’ shape-shifting in response to environmental changes complicates daylight simulation. Therefore, predicting the indoor daylight environment based on the morphological changes of dynamic facades is beneficial for the design optimization and intelligent control of the facades. This paper introduces a multimodal fusion learning model for predicting the hourly indoor daylight environment of dynamic facades. We created a parametric generation model for dynamic facades. After simulating daylight uniformity, we constructed a multi-modal dataset that includes image data and numerical data. Subsequently, we applied ResNet-50 to extract image features and Multilayer Perceptron to extract numerical data features. After performing feature fusion, we utilized a neural network to establish a regression prediction model. The results show that the multimodal fusion prediction model is superior to the single-modal model. The multimodal fusion model with an attention module to extract image features, demonstrates the best predictive performance, with an R2 value reaching 0.92 on the training set and 0.90 on the test set. © 2025 and published by the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong.
